\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Complementarity in a stated balanced model.} Consider a horizontally periodic slab $0<z<H$ and the linear quasigeostrophic relations
\[
 b=f_0\partial_z\psi,\qquad q=\Delta_h\psi+\partial_z(S(z)\partial_z\psi),
\]
where $f_0\ne0$, $S(z)>0$ is smooth, $\psi$ is a streamfunction and $b$ is the buoyancy proxy for temperature. Knowing $b$ yields $\psi(x,y,z)=\psi_0(x,y)+f_0^{-1}\int_0^z b(x,y,s)ds$. Thus temperature fixes vertical shear but leaves the barotropic function $\psi_0$ undetermined. Adding any nonconstant horizontal $\chi(x,y)$ leaves $b$ unchanged and changes $q$ by $\Delta_h\chi$.

\paragraph{What PV plus boundary data supplies.} Suppose two smooth solutions have the same $q$ and the same normal derivative at top and bottom. Their difference $h$ satisfies $\Delta_hh+\partial_z(S\partial_zh)=0$ and homogeneous normal conditions. Multiplying by $h$ and integrating by parts gives
\[
 \int(|\nabla_hh|^2+S|\partial_zh|^2)=0.
\]
Therefore $h$ is constant on the connected slab. Fixing the spatial mean makes the inversion unique. The boundary terms are essential; dropping them invalidates the argument.

\paragraph{Remaining breadth.} This proves a precise information complementarity in one chosen linear balance: temperature/shear data and PV constrain different components. It does not establish a general relation between the source's dry potential-temperature inversion and nonlinear Ertel-PV inversion in every regime. Hydrostatic boundary pressure, nonlinear balance and possible degeneracies must be specified. Extending the stated uniqueness argument to those physical formulations is the first gap.

